@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+# Minecraft pre-batch references
+
+Immutable production excerpts from core `b697d1536409575c03c2b5a12ed6d3d031bfefda`, captured before this batch. `SHA256.json` records their byte digests. The runners extract the current candidate separately and never regenerate the baseline. Namespace renaming and test-only stubs isolate dependencies; native `simple_array`, `v128`, descriptor field definitions, queue packets, lockless queue and atomics remain in the relevant fixtures.
+
+`run-minecraft-optimization-tests.py` executes reservation, cache, validation and descriptor state comparisons. `run-minecraft-vertex-tests.py` executes full layout analysis/packing/range routines and type helpers; fixtures control RSX registers and guest memory. The descriptor fixture executes all three production binding routines and comparisons. The index fixture retains the native queue and packet implementation, replacing only its notification plumbing; it checks overlapping queued jobs and concurrent consumers. DMA tests use the independent per-vector oracle, including overlapping virtual ranges, physical aliases and inaccessible page bounds, plus the existing independent seeded XXH3 oracle. Fence tests extract real wait/signal methods; `--native-atomic` compiles the production wait engine and supplies only formatting/error/main-thread plumbing.
+
+Run each Python runner normally or with `--sanitize`; `--benchmark` alternates old/new order, excludes two warmups and reports ten-repeat medians. Fence benchmarks report waiting CPU and wake latency separately. Mac/ARM host measurements establish component performance, not iPhone FPS. References cover test domains, not a proof of all emulator behavior. The full contract runner includes the new paths; the existing IOSFPSBatch fixture covers DMA and hashing. See the parent diagnostics report for accepted/rejected candidates, validation and the physical checkpoint.
